<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yadava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yadavas, Yadu dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yadavas are an ancient lineage of Indian tradition, descended from the legendary king Yadu. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> they are the clans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and Dwarka to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> belonged. Many later dynasties and communities, among them the Seuna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadavas of Devagiri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> houses such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadaun">Jadauns</a> and the Varmans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, claimed Yadava descent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yadava.jpg" alt="Yadava" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uwe Dedering · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_tribes_between_the_Indus_and_the_Ganges.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ancient Indian literature lists the Yadava clans as branches of the lineage of Yadu, a king of the Chandravamsha line. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haihaya">Haihayas</a> were believed to descend from Sahasrajit, Yadu's elder son, and the other clans, among them the Chedis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbhas</a>, Satvatas, Andhakas, Kukuras, Bhojas, Vrishnis and Surasenas, from his younger son Kroshtu. The Haihayas were a confederacy of five clans that later took the name of the strongest, the Vitihotras; in the Puranas Arjuna Kartavirya took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahishmati">Mahishmati</a> from Karkotaka Naga and made it his capital. The genealogies of several Puranas suggest that the Yadavas spread across the Aravalli region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> valley, the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> describe the Yadavas as a confederacy of pastoral cowherd clans ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> region. In the Puranas, Kamsa usurped the throne of Mathura by imprisoning his father Ugrasena of the Kukura clan and was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, son of Devaki, who restored Ugrasena. The Mahabharata tells of an exodus from Mathura to Dvaraka under pressure from the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, and of a fratricidal war a few years after the Kurukshetra War in which the Andhaka and Vrishni clans of Dvaraka destroyed one another. Balarama and Krishna died soon after, and the survivors took refuge in Indraprastha, where Vajra, Krishna's great-grandson, became their king. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> of the Pali canon tell the same story.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>'s Ashtadhyayi counts the Satvatas, Andhakas and Vrishnis among clans with a sangha, a tribal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Kautilya</a>'s Arthaśāstra says the leader of the Kukuras used the title of rājā. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romila-thapar">Romila Thapar</a> sees traces of a matrilineal structure in the cross-cousin marriages recorded for the Yadavas. The Mora Well Inscription near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, of the early decades of the Common Era, records a shrine to five Vrishni heroes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> reported that the Shurasenas of Mathura worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Herakles</a>, possibly meaning Vasudeva Krishna.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yadava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jadaun Rajputs, claiming descent from the <b>Yadavas</b>, ruled around Bayana and Timangarh before the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/karauli-state">Karauli State</a></li><li><blockquote>Surasena emerged around Mathura on the Yamuna river, a region linked in tradition with the <b>Yadava</b> clans.</blockquote><a href="/state/surasena">Surasena</a></li><li><blockquote>The Varmans claimed descent from a <b>Yadava</b> family of Simhapura, probably in Kalinga.</blockquote><a href="/state/varman-dynasty-of-bengal">Varman dynasty of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
